  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 917 PM MDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nSCATTERED STORMS SUNDAY: \n\nShower and storm coverage increases tomorrow as the next strong\npush of moisture begins to move in. PWATs continue to climb, \nwith values upwards of 200% of normal, with the likely chance of\nexceeding the 75th percentile and approaching, if not \nsurpassing the max values for mid September. \n\n\nCOOLER AND WETTER PATTERN MONDAY AND BEYOND: \n\nBy Monday, a deep trough begins to push into the PacNW. Due to \ncyclonic flow around a low, southwesterly flow will be favored \nover the southwestern CONUS. This means subtropical moisture \nwill be pushed further inland, centering the strong plume of \nmoisture over our CWA. PWATs continue to be forecasted over the \n75th percentile for this time of year, which will be enough to \nprompt our next chance for widespread showers and storms.\n\nMonday is the peak of the moisture, but it may be so much that \nwe end up cloudy therefore inhibiting instability. If that \nhappens we will see more stratiform showers. However, it is \nworth considering the dynamic forcing at play during this time \nperiod. Firstly, Sunday night into Monday will see a cold \nfrontal passage which will include pockets of frontogenesis. \nSecondly, by Monday we are expected to be under the right \nentrance region of an upper level jet. This may enhance the lift\nacross the area. Also, the jet may provide wind shear that \ncould support strong to severe storms capable of hail and \ndamaging winds. Given the moisture there is the potential for \nheavy rainfall as well. Cell training and or any severe storm \ncould lead to localized flash flooding.\n\nDespite the shortwave ejecting out over the Northern Plains the\nmodels show a trough remaining in place across the PacNW as \nwell as the upper level jet overhead. The highly anamolous \nmoisture also pushes east, but there is still sufficient \nmoisture lingering across the area. The shear and moisture may \nonce again could support strong storms. The coverage is more \nuncertain with the lack of forcing, so the convection may be \nmore terrain driven. Confidence decreases mid week as the second\nPacNW trough either swings through or becomes a cut off low to \nour west. \n\nLook for daytime highs to cool down Monday and beyond due to \nthat aforementioned frontal passage as well as the cloud cover \nand potential showers.",
